# Cloudflare Pages security headers (production).
#
# The CSP here mirrors the production policy injected into index.html by
# vite.config.ts (cspPlugin), but as a real HTTP response header it can also
# carry directives a <meta> tag cannot — notably frame-ancestors (clickjacking
# protection). If you change one, change the other so they stay in sync.
#
# The app runs no server, so a crafted file can't beacon anywhere (#182). The one
# third party is Google Drive (lib/google-drive.ts): its sign-in and Picker are
# scripts Google serves, the Picker is a docs.google.com iframe, GIS injects a
# stylesheet from its origin, and file contents go to googleapis.com. The meta
# policy gates those hosts on the Drive env vars; production is configured, so
# this header carries them. Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy must stay
# `same-origin-allow-popups`: GIS's sign-in popup posts the token back through
# window.opener, and plain `same-origin` severs that so sign-in never completes.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://accounts.google.com https://apis.google.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://accounts.google.com; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self' https://www.googleapis.com https://accounts.google.com https://apis.google.com; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'; frame-src https://accounts.google.com https://docs.google.com; frame-ancestors 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups

# The service worker and manifest must always be revalidated, never served stale
# from the HTTP cache — otherwise a new deploy's SW (with its bumped cache name)
# and updated icon URLs can't propagate to an installed PWA. `no-cache` = "check
# with the server before using the cached copy" (not "don't store").
/sw.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache
/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache
